---
import Base from "../../layouts/Base.astro";

const updated = "November 6, 2026";
const repo = "rt-labs-dev/rt-wow-sync-releases";
---

<Base title="rt-wow-sync privacy policy" description="How rt-wow-sync handles your game settings and your Google Drive data.">
  <article
    class="max-w-2xl space-y-4 py-16 text-neutral-300 [&_a]:text-cyan-300 [&_a:hover]:underline [&_h2]:mt-10 [&_h2]:text-lg [&_h2]:font-semibold [&_h2]:text-neutral-100 [&_li]:ml-4 [&_ul]:list-disc [&_ul]:space-y-1.5 [&_ul]:marker:text-cyan-400"
  >
    <h1 class="text-3xl font-semibold tracking-tight text-neutral-50">rt-wow-sync privacy policy</h1>
    <p class="font-mono text-[11px] text-neutral-500">Last updated {updated}</p>

    <p>
      rt-wow-sync keeps World of Warcraft addon settings in sync between your computers. This policy explains what data it
      handles, where that data goes, and how you control it. The short version: your settings go only to the sync location
      you choose, which is a folder on your computer or your own Google Drive. We do not run servers that receive your
      data, and the app contains no analytics, advertising or tracking.
    </p>

    <h2>What the app reads and creates</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>
        It reads the <code class="text-neutral-100">WTF/Account</code> folder of each World of Warcraft flavor you have
        installed. That folder holds addon saved settings, per-character settings, keybindings and macros. It never
        reads your game login or your account password.
      </li>
      <li>
        If you turn on <strong class="text-neutral-100">Sync addons too</strong>, it also reads and syncs the
        <code class="text-neutral-100">Interface/AddOns</code> folder, which holds the addons themselves. This is off by
        default.
      </li>
      <li>
        A sync creates a snapshot: a zip file of those settings (and addons, if enabled), plus details used to compare snapshots. The details are
        the name you give this computer in Settings (by default its computer name), the time, the file count, size and a
        content hash, and a list of the addons installed on that computer.
      </li>
      <li>
        Before a pull or restore replaces your files, the app saves a backup of the current files on your computer, including addons when addon sync is on. Old
        snapshots and backups are removed automatically, keeping the most recent ones.
      </li>
      <li>
        The app's settings, backups and Google sign-in are stored in its settings folder on your computer:
        <code class="text-neutral-100">%APPDATA%\rt-wow-sync</code> on Windows and
        <code class="text-neutral-100">~/Library/Application Support/rt-wow-sync</code> on macOS.
      </li>
    </ul>

    <h2>Where snapshots go</h2>
    <p>You choose one sync location in the app's settings.</p>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <strong class="text-neutral-100">A folder.</strong> Snapshots are written to a folder you pick. If that folder is
        synced by another service, such as Google Drive for desktop, Dropbox or OneDrive, that service's own privacy
        policy applies to it. rt-wow-sync does not connect to any service in this mode.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong class="text-neutral-100">Google Drive.</strong> Snapshots are uploaded to a folder named "WoW Sync" in your
        own My Drive, where you can see and manage them like any other file. They are not encrypted by the app, and are
        protected by your Google account.
      </li>
    </ul>

    <h2>Google Drive access</h2>
    <p>
      Google Drive sync is off until you sign in with Google in the app's settings. The app then requests one permission,
      <code class="text-neutral-100">drive.file</code>, which only gives it access to files it created itself. It cannot
      see, open or change any of your other Drive files.
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li>Your data is transferred directly between your computer and your Google Drive.</li>
      <li>The app only communicates with Google's sign-in and Google Drive services.</li>
      <li>
        Your Google sign-in is saved in the app's settings folder on your computer, protected by your operating system
        account.
      </li>
    </ul>

    <h2>How Google user data is used</h2>
    <p>
      rt-wow-sync uses access to your Google Drive only to store and retrieve your own game setting snapshots for your own
      computers. The data is not sold, not used for advertising, not used to train AI models, and not shared with anyone.
      rt-wow-sync's use of information received from Google APIs adheres to the{" "}
      <a href="https://developers.google.com/terms/api-services-user-data-policy">Google API Services User Data Policy</a>,
      including the Limited Use requirements.
    </p>

    <h2>Removing your data</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>Delete the "WoW Sync" folder in your Google Drive, or the sync folder you chose, to remove all snapshots.</li>
      <li>
        <strong class="text-neutral-100">Sign out</strong> in the app's settings removes the saved Google sign-in from that
        computer. To revoke rt-wow-sync's access entirely, remove it at{" "}
        <a href="https://myaccount.google.com/permissions">your Google Account permissions</a>.
      </li>
      <li>Uninstalling the app and deleting its settings folder removes its settings and local backups.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>This website</h2>
    <p>
      This site has no cookies, analytics or tracking. It is hosted on GitHub Pages, and installers are downloaded from
      GitHub. GitHub may log technical information such as IP addresses as part of operating those services, as described
      in{" "}
      <a href="https://docs.github.com/site-policy/privacy-policies/github-general-privacy-statement">GitHub's privacy statement</a>.
    </p>

    <h2>Changes and contact</h2>
    <p>
      If this policy changes, the new version will be posted on this page with a new date. For questions or problems,
      open an issue at{" "}
      <a href={`https://github.com/${repo}/issues`}>github.com/{repo}</a>.
    </p>
  </article>
</Base>
